// examples (no patient data; initials and dates are made up here), and a generated R3 logbook.

export const DEMO_REFLECTIONS = [ { "headingId": "thyroid", "subId": null, "initials": "QT", "diagnosis": "Right neck dissection", "title": "Surgery In A Patient With Recent Stroke And Cerebrovascular Disease", "summary": "This was a 71-year-old man who was planned for undergoing right neck dissection for recurrent papillary thyroid carcinoma (right nodal recurrence). He had a significant medical history of hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, and a recent acute ischaemic stroke 6 months ago (completed dual antiplatelet therapy). MRI/MRA showed severe steno-occlusive disease involving bilateral anterior cerebral arteries and the left middle cerebral artery (M1 segment). Neurology consult was requested by the preoperative anaesthesia clinic, impression was elevated but not prohibitive for surgery, and suggested a systolic blood pressure target of 120-150 intraoperatively. Intraoperatively, cerebral oximetry was used for monitoring of regional cerebral oxygenation. Baseline rSO2 was 58 on the left and 57 on the right on room air, and baseline blood pressure was 147/84. Careful induction with phenylephrine induction was carried out, and lowest systolic blood pressure post-induction was 124. After knife to skin, it was noted that bilateral cerebral oximetry values suddenly dropped to 51-52 despite stable haemodynamics and oxygenation, possibly due to increased metabolic demand from increased stimulation. Depth of anaesthesia was optimised, but as patient state index was already less than 25, other factors were optimised. FiO2 was progressively increased to 50%, and blood pressure was kept around systolic 130s, both of which improved the cerebral oximetry values to baseline. No further cerebral oximetry desaturations were noted although surgery took longer due to increased surgical bleeding, but deemed manageable by the surgeons. The patient was extubated awake and was able to move all 4 limbs in the recovery area with no new neurological deficit.", "points": [ { "heading": "Timing of surgery in recent stroke", "text": "Patients with recent stroke are at high risk of perioperative cerebrovascular accidents. As per the 2024 AHA/ASA guidelines [1], postoperative ischaemic stroke risk is high within the first 3 months of a recent stroke or transient ischaemic attack. This risk continues to level off until 9 months after the stroke, but does not return to baseline. This helps us decide on the timing of surgery and counsel the patient appropriately. However, this will need to be balanced with the risks of delaying surgery, such as worsening of cancer prognosis. Given the time-sensitive nature of this surgery, proceeding with surgery after 3 months is an acceptable compromise although this should be discussed with the surgeons and the neurologist." }, { "heading": "Blood pressure targets", "text": "While the 2021 AHA/ASA guidelines recommend MAP >70 mmHg to maintain end-organ perfusion and avoid cerebral ischaemic in patients with recent stroke [2], the neurologist recommended 120-150, which was within 20% of his baseline blood pressure. Our patient’s regional cerebral oxygenation did drop when his systolic blood pressure was below 130, and generally blood pressure targets should be individualised." }, { "heading": "The role of cerebral oximetry", "text": "Cerebral oximetry provides real-time monitoring of regional cerebral perfusion, and can guide intraoperative haemodynamic and oxygenation management in these high-risk patients. In our patient, it helped to guide blood pressure and FiO2 targets. Although studies on cerebral oximetry have mostly focused on cardiac surgery, it can also be useful to guide management in select high-risk noncardiac cases. However, it must be noted that there is no high-quality evidence that cerebral oximetry reduces postoperative stroke risk [3]." } ], "references": [ "Benesch C, Glance LG, Derdeyn CP, et al. Perioperative Neurological Evaluation and Management to Lower the Risk of Acute Stroke in Patients Undergoing Noncardiac, Nonneurological Surgery: A Scientific Statement From the American Heart Association/American Stroke Association. Circulation. 2021;143(19):e923-e946.", "Thompson A, Fleischmann KE, Smilowitz NR, et al. 2024 AHA/ACC/ACS/ASNC/HRS/SCA/SCCT/SCMR/SVM Guideline for Perioperative Cardiovascular Management for Noncardiac Surgery: A Report of the American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association Joint Committee on Clinical Practice Guidelines. Circulation. 2024;150(19):e351-e442.", "Yu Y, Zhang K, Zhang L, Zong H, Meng L, Han R. Cerebral near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) for perioperative monitoring of brain oxygenation in children and adults. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2018;1(1.:CD010947." ] }, { "headingId": "thyroid", "subId": null, "initials": "RX", "diagnosis": "Right canal wall down mastoidectomy and ossicular chain reconstruction", "title": "Middle Ear Surgery And Postoperative Nausea And Vomiting", "summary": "Patient was a 25-year-old lady, 41kg in weight (Body mass index 19). She presented with right ear discharge for several months, and was diagnosed with right external auditory canal cholesteatoma involving the middle ear with ossicular chain disruption and mastoid air cell involvement. She had no significant past medical or surgical history and was a non-smoker. She was also noted to easily get car-sickness in the preoperative evaluation. She was planned for right canal wall down mastoidectomy and ossicular chain reconstruction as a same day admission, with facial nerve monitoring.\n\nIntraoperatively, patient was induced and orally intubated with a reinforced tube uneventfully. The tube was swung to the left. Anaesthesia was maintained using total intravenous anaesthesia (TIVA) and remifentanil. Dexamethasone and ondansetron were given, together with multimodal analgesia including paracetamol and parecoxib. Short-acting atracurium was given for intubation but was subsequently not topped up. At the end of the procedure, a total of 2mg of oxycodone was given, and patient was extubated on remifentanil.\n\nPostoperatively, patient had mild pain with a pain score of 3 but was bearable and did not require additional postoperative opioids. She did not complain of any nausea or vomiting. Postoperative pain in the ward was well controlled with paracetamol and etoricoxib, and patient was discharged uneventfully.", "points": [ { "heading": "Postoperative nausea and vomiting after middle ear surgery", "text": "Middle ear surgeries are highly emetogenic due to manipulation and irritation of the vestibular system. Additionally, being a young female non-smoker placed her Apfel score at 2, with 4 risk factors. As per the recent 2025 5th Consensus Guidelines for PONV by ASER [1], she is at high risk for PONV, and at least 3 pharmacological interventions to reduce PONV should be instituted. For this patient, we used TIVA, ondansetron and dexamethasone to reduce her PONV risk. Dexmedetomidine may also be used. Other pharmacological rescue options available postoperatively include metoclopramide, droperidol (Low doses of 0.625 mg have lower risk of arrhythmias), haloperidol, and promethazine. The guidelines also recognise the use of nonpharmacological options such as PC6 acupoint stimulation, as well as aromatherapy. Palonosetron, a longer acting 5-HT3 antagonist, provides longer acting PONV prophylaxis and reduces post-discharge nausea and vomiting (PDNV), although it is prudent to handover its administration to the surgical team to ensure ondansetron is not served for the next 24 hours. Overall, combination therapy is recommended over monotherapy, especially for high-risk patients." } ], "references": [ "Gan TJ, Jin Z, Ayad S, et al. Fifth Consensus Guidelines for the Management of Postoperative Nausea and Vomiting: Executive Summary. Anesth Analg. Published online November 14, 2025." ] }, { "headingId": "lap", "subId": null, "initials": "YV", "diagnosis": "Laparoscopic", "title": "Severe Obstructive Sleep Apnoea (OSA)", "summary": "This patient was a 46-year-old lady (92kg, 1.63m, body mass index of 34.6) scheduled for elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy for gallstones. Significant past medical history included severe obstructive sleep apnoea (OSA) on polysomnography (Apnoea-Hypopnoea Index 46) on continuous positive airway pressure therapy (CPAP). Other significant past medical history included hypertension. On the surgery day, she did not bring her CPAP machine. Rapid sequence induction with cricoid pressure was performed with propofol, fentanyl and succinylcholine. Laryngeal grade was 2B on videolaryngoscopy requiring external laryngeal pressure and bougie. Post-induction subcostal transversus abdominis plane (TAP) block was performed. Intraoperative course was uneventful, and patient was given a total of 150mcg of fentanyl. Patient was extubated fully awake, head up on the trolley. She was monitored for 1 hour in the recovery, and was sent to the general ward on 2 litres nasal prongs with overnight continuous SpO2 monitoring. She was restarted on her CPAP in the general ward after her family brought the machine to the ward.", "points": [ { "heading": "Preoperative assessment of OSA", "text": "The severity of OSA is graded by AHI, and high scores indicate higher risk of difficult airway and post-operative desaturation. While the STOPBANG score may be useful as a clinical screening questionnaire, a sleep study remains the gold standard and should be considered especially if it will change anaesthetic management. CPAP should be continued perioperatively, and patients should be instructed to bring their CPAP on day of procedure. If the patient is keen, preoperative initiation of CPAP will reduce perioperative risks especially in the setting of elective surgery. Cardiovascular status and comorbidities should also be screened for, as patient’s with OSA often have various cardiovascular derangements such as hypertension." }, { "heading": "Airway management in OSA", "text": "OSA is associated with crowding of oropharyngeal structures, which increase the difficulty of bag-mask ventilation and laryngoscopy. The airway should be evaluated during preoperative assessment, and there should be a lower threshold for awake tracheal intubation if there is further evidence of a difficult airway, or a history of difficult airway. Comorbidities such as obesity will reduce the safe apnoea time and increase the physiological difficult of the airway. Premedication should be given with caution. Similarly, extubation should be performed awake and with the patient sitting up. If the patient’s CPAP is available, extubating to CPAP can help to splint airway structures and reduce the risk of acute airway obstruction postoperatively." }, { "heading": "Postoperative considerations in OSA", "text": "Generally, regional anaesthesia is preferred to avoid the sedative effects of general anaesthesia and difficult airway. If general anaesthesia is performed, opioid-sparing techniques should be instituted to reduce the risk of opioid-induced respiratory depression. The effects of general anaesthesia can last many hours, and continuous monitoring postoperatively, with supplemental oxygen administration, is recommended, especially in the first 24 hours after surgery. Day surgery is generally contraindicated in severe OSA, and high-dependency monitoring may be required. Patients should be monitored for longer in the post-anaesthesia care unit until the effects of anaesthesia have completely worn off, and attendant respiratory risks have reduced." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "lap", "subId": null, "initials": "ZK", "diagnosis": "Laparoscopic right", "title": "Patient Blood Management in Colorectal Surgery", "summary": "The first patient was a 68-year-old gentleman with hypertension, hyperlipidaemia and minor coronary artery disease on aspirin. He was planned for laparoscopic right hemicolectomy for ascending colon adenocarcinoma, and was seen as an inpatient anaesthesia blue letter for optimisation. He was noted to have a preoperative haemoglobin of 9.7. Iron panel was sent off that revealed iron deficiency anaemia. Intravenous Ferinject was given. Patient was then discharged and proceeded for his surgery 2 weeks from discharge.\n\nThe second patient was a 73-year-old lady with newly diagnosed splenic flexure colorectal cancer. Significant medical history included left subsegmental pulmonary embolism on clexane (stopped pre-op), symptomatic 2:1 atrioventricular block on pacemaker, hypertension and hyperlipidaemia. Notably, she had a preoperative haemoglobin of 9.1. She was given Ferinject, and surgery proceeded while she was still inpatient (1 week after Ferinject).", "points": [ { "heading": "Pillars of patient blood management (PBM)", "text": "Anaemia, bleeding, and transfusion are all independent risk factors for adverse patient outcomes. Patient blood management aims to reduce the need for transfusion through three pillars: Optimising erythropoiesis, minimizing blood loss, and improving patients reserves of anaemia. These are implemented preoperatively, intraoperative and postoperatively." }, { "heading": "Route of iron supplementation", "text": "Iron deficiency anaemia is common among colorectal patients, and should be screened for and optimised preoperatively, as per the 2025 ERAS Colorectal guidelines. Intravenous iron is especially preferable in patients who have a short window for optimisation before surgery (<4 weeks), who are undergoing major surgery with moderate to large amounts of blood loss (>500ml) or have moderate to severe anaemia (haemoglobin < 10 g/dL). While iron stores are quickly repleted, increase in haemoglobin may only be evident in 1-2 weeks, hence surgery that is scheduled in <2 weeks may need to be postponed. However, it is never too late to start treatment of preoperative anaemia, and preoperative correction will help in postoperative transfusion rates." }, { "heading": "Intraoperative management", "text": "While surgical technique is a key factor in intraoperative blood loss, anaesthetists play a role in minimizing and correcting coagulopathy. This includes avoiding hypothermia, acidosis and hypocalcaemia, as well as using point-of-care testing to evaluate clotting function." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "cabg", "subId": null, "initials": "WP", "diagnosis": "Coronary artery bypass surgery (CABG)", "title": "Functional Iron Deficiency Anaemia", "summary": "This was a 77-year-old lady admitted for coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG). She had ischaemic heart disease with triple vessel disease (distal LAD 80%, distal LCx 100%, mRCA 80%), hypertension, diabetes, and chronic kidney disease. Preoperative assessment was largely unremarkable (ejection fraction 55%, carotid ultrasound normal, CT thorax done, no loose teeth). I saw her in the preoperative clinic, counselled her for surgery and transoesophageal echocardiography, and cleared her for surgery. Her haemoglobin was 9.2, but she had a recent iron panel a few months ago which showed high ferritin 272, hence I did not request for anything additional. However, after discussion with the primary anaesthetist, I realised that her transferrin saturation (Tsat) was 10.1%, which meant that she had functional iron deficiency despite normal ferritin. On reflection, she should have gotten intravenous iron Ferinject preoperatively in line with institutional guidelines.", "points": [ { "heading": "Absolute vs Functional Iron Deficiency Anaemia", "text": "Absolute iron deficiency is diagnosed when serum ferritin is less than 30 ug/ml. However, ferritin is an acute phase reactant and is elevated in inflammatory states. Additionally, hepcidin sequesters iron into macrophages, resulting in insufficient iron mobilisation for erythropoiesis even if iron body stores are normal. In these cases, Tsat (serum iron divided by total iron-binding capacity) can provide an indication of the availability of circulating iron in the blood to tissues, with a low Tsat of <20% indicating functional iron deficiency. Reticulocyte haemoglobin equivalent (Ret-HE) is a newer marker that is more sensitive and less affected by inflammation, and indicates the haemoglobin content of developing red-blood cells (reticulocytes). Even in the presence of adequate Tsat >20%, some patients with ferritin <500 ug/L can still benefit from iron supplementation if their Ret-HE is <30 pg." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "cabg", "subId": null, "initials": "JH", "diagnosis": "Coronary artery bypass surgery (CABG)", "title": "Arrhythmia On Rewarming", "summary": "Patient was a 70-year-old gentleman who presented with triple vessel disease (Coronary angiography: LAD 90%, D1 90%, RI 90%, LCx vestigial, RCA 90%. Transthoracic echocardiography: ejection fraction 40%, multiple regional wall motion abnormalities). Significant past medical history included hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, diabetes on oral hypoglycaemia agents, and asthma. He was listed for coronary artery bypass graft x3. Intraoperatively, midazolam and fentanyl boluses were given for line insertion. Thereafter, general anaesthesia was induced with midazolam, propofol, fentanyl and rocuronium, with noradrenaline running. Transoesophageal echography was performed, and surgery proceeded.  Patient was heparinized, ACT was 586s, and cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB) initiated. During CPB, hyperglycaemia was noted requiring 4U/hr of Actrapid. On rewarming, patent developed ventricular fibrillation that required one defibrillation. Pacing wires were inserted and patient was weaned off CPB on noradrenaline 0.05 mcg/kg/min. The chest was closed and patient was sent to ICU on noradrenaline 0.01 mcg/kg/min", "points": [ { "heading": "Management of arrythmias during rewarming", "text": "During rewarming, the heart becomes significantly irritable and may lead to arrhythmias. Bradycardia and heart block, as well as tachyarrhythmias and atrial fibrillation may occur. Therefore, electrolytes should be corrected (especially hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia), and the surgeon may choose to insert epicardial pacing wires to maintain adequate heart rate during weaning of CPB and postoperatively. Ventricular fibrillation is also frequently seen, as with this patient, and immediate internal defibrillation is indicated." }, { "heading": "Perioperative glycaemic control in cardiac surgery", "text": "Cardiac surgery and cardiopulmonary bypass induce high physiological stresses that triggers insulin resistance and hyperglycaemia. This is significantly more prevalent in diabetics, who often require continuous intravenous insulin infusion to maintain euglycemia. Close monitoring of glucose is important as insulin sensitivity improves upon rewarming, potentially leading to hypoglycaemia." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "cardiac", "subId": null, "initials": "FD", "diagnosis": "Transcatheter aortic valve implantation (TAVI)", "title": "Management of Transcatheter Aortic Valve Implantation (TAVI)", "summary": "The first patient was a 67-year-old lady with severe symptomatic calcific aortic stenosis (Vmax 4.18 m/s, AVA 0.5 cm2. She also primary biliary cirrhosis and chronic anaemia (Hb 10.6). Otherwise, ejection fraction was preserved at 60%. She was listed for Transcatheter aortic valve implantation (TAVI) under monitored anaesthetic care. Pacing pads were placed preoperatively. She was loaded with dexmedetomidine 1mcg/kg, then continued on an infusion. A left radial arterial line and right internal jugular central venous catheter was inserted. Titrated fentanyl boluses were given during stimulating parts such as groin puncture. Rapid ventricular pacing was done during the balloon valvuloplasty and valve deployment, which was accompanied by transient haemodynamic instability. After successful deployment of the valve, transthoracic echocardiography was done to confirm placement, the patient was transferred to the coronary care unit.\n\nThe second patient was a 76-year-old male with severe aortic stenosis (AVA 0.67 cm2. and ischemic cardiomyopathy (ejection fraction 30%) with a previous coronary artery bypass graft. As he was frailer, we sedated with dexmedetomidine 0.5 mcg/kg/h without loading, and supplemented it with 1mg of midazolam during line insertion. A left radial arterial line and right internal jugular central venous catheter was inserted. Adrenaline was on standby during rapid ventricular pacing, but blood pressure improved with phenylephrine boluses. Post-deployment, blood pressure increased dramatically requiring glyceryl trinitrate infusion. He was sent to the coronary care unit postoperatively uneventfully.", "points": [ { "heading": "Haemodynamic management in TAVI", "text": "It is critically important to maintain haemodynamic stability in TAVI, as these patients are at high cardiac risk with limited reserves. The haemodynamic goal in aortic stenosis is “slow, full and tight”, and vasopressors should be connected and on standby. During rapid ventricular pacing and valve deployment, transient hypotension is common, and it is important to have phenylephrine on standby. Adrenaline may be required if the patient collapses after pacing. After graft deployment, sudden relief of aortic stenosis can also lead to hypertension requiring antihypertensives." }, { "heading": "General anaesthesia vs sedation for TAVI", "text": "General anaesthesia provides a motionless surgical field and allows for apnoea during valve deployment. Sedation also relies of transthoracic echocardiography to assess valve function and for complications, which is inferior to transoesophageal echocardiography. However there has been a shift to sedation due to less haemodynamic instability, faster recovery, and earlier detection of periprocedural stroke. Recently, the latest 5-year follow-up of the landmark SOLVE-TAVI Trial [1] showed that although there was no difference in composite endpoint, the sedation group had a lower all-cause mortality. While it did not standardize sedation strategy, it provided guidance to use propofol, dexmedetomidine or another nonbenzodiazepine agent. In my experience, dexmedetomidine is especially useful as it provides both sedation and analgesia but without significant respiratory depression." } ], "references": [ "Feistritzer HJ, Kurz T, Vonthein R, et al. Effect of Valve Type and Anesthesia Strategy for TAVR: 5-Year Results of the SOLVE-TAVI Trial. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2025;85(1):74-82." ] }, { "headingId": "cardiac", "subId": null, "initials": "UN", "diagnosis": "Ablation of ventricular tachycardia", "title": "Ventricular Tachycardia Ablation", "summary": "The patient was a 51-year-old male who had recurrent epicardial LV summit VT, with three previous ablations 2016, 2021, and 2022, all of which were unsuccessful, and was on maintenance amiodarone and bisoprolol. He had a Cardiac Implantable Electronic Device (CIED) in the left infraclavicular area, and the last interrogation in Feb 2025 still revealed a treated ventricular fibrillation episode requiring a 135J shock. Additionally, he also had bicuspid aortic valve with mild-to-moderate aortic regurgitation, severe left ventricular dysfunction (ejection fraction 30%). Latest thyroid function was unremarkable. The proceduralist warned us that this would be extremely challenging and might be more than 4 hours, and that general anaesthesia would hence be required. After arterial line insertion pre-induction, anaesthesia was induced and maintained using total intravenous propofol (to avoid volatiles) and remifentanil (for titratability). Central venous catheter was then inserted for inotropic and vasopressor support. During the procedure, there were periods where ventricular tachycardia would need to be induced to allow for ablation, and the patient tolerated those poorly. Additionally, minimal fluids were given as saline flushes were continuously used by the proceduralist, and totalled over 1 litre. Postoperatively, he was extubated but could not be weaned off noradrenaline, and was sent to the coronary care unit on vasopressor support.", "points": [ { "heading": "Choice of anaesthetic for ventricular tachycardia ablation", "text": "While general anaesthesia theoretically can suppress the arrhythmogenic area (making ablation harder) and cause my hypotension, GA is often required due the prolonged nature of VT ablation and need for immobility and cardioversion. Propofol is preferred as avoids the QTc prolongation associated with sevoflurane which affects the ablation." }, { "heading": "Myocardial depression and fluid balance", "text": "Our patient was not able to be weaned off noradrenaline, likely due to repeated VT induction and cardioversion causing myocardial depression, on a baseline of EF of 30%. Notably, as the whole procedure took about 4 hours, the cumulative saline flush from the ablation catheters was significant. Thankfully, blood loss was minimal <100ml in our case, but can also be significant in prolonged cases." } ], "references": [ "Mittnacht AJ, Dukkipati S, Mahajan A. Ventricular tachycardia ablation: a comprehensive review for anesthesiologists. Anesth Analg. 2015;120(4):737-748." ] }, { "headingId": "vascular", "subId": null, "initials": "KM", "diagnosis": "Above knee amputation (AKA)", "title": "Above-Knee Amputation In A Septic, Haemodynamically Unstable Patient", "summary": "This was a 72-year-old man admitted for left lower limb gas gangrene requiring urgent above-knee amputation (AKA). He had background history of diabetes mellitus, peripheral arterial disease on dual antiplatelets, poor oral intake with hyponatraemia (Na 128 mmol/L), adrenal insufficiency already on stress-dose steroids, and normocytic normochromic anaemia (Hb 8.4). Latest transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) had showed poor left ventricular systolic function of 35%. He was just on peripheral noradrenaline yesterday but had been weaned off preoperatively. As he was at high risk of haemodynamic instability, decision was made to do the procedure under peripheral nerve block and minimal sedation. Four nerve blocks were done: femoral nerve, lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN), obturator nerve, and subgluteal sciatic nerve blocks. 0.4% ropivacaine was used to reduce total dose of local anaesthetic to within to toxic limit. Despite adequate blockade, 20mg of ketamine and 50mcg of fentanyl was still required intraoperatively for sedation. 1 pint of packed red cells was also given in view of blood loss. The patient remained haemodynamically stable throughout the procedure, only requiring a low dose phenylephrine infusion during periods of bleeding that responded to transfusion of blood.", "points": [ { "heading": "Peripheral nerve blocks for high-risk patients", "text": "In critically ill, haemodynamically unstable patients with high cardiovascular risks, general anaesthesia or neuraxial anaesthesia may induce significant hypotension. Performing the surgery under peripheral nerve blocks alone can provide more haemodynamic stability if performed well. However, a poorly working nerve block can also lead to significant pain and sympathetic stimulation, which may in itself stress the cardiovascular system. If the block is assessed to be patchy or incomplete, a gentle controlled general anaesthesia may be more appropriate from the get-go." }, { "heading": "Surgical anaesthesia for AKA", "text": "Adequate surgical anaesthesia for an AKA is difficult to achieve due to the multiple overlapping innervations of the surgical area, requiring blockade of femoral, lateral femoral cutaneous, obturator, and sciatic nerves for complete coverage. When performing multiple nerve blocks, the cumulative dose of local anaesthetic can exceed the toxic dose, hence doses should be precalculated and the concentration appropriately reduced if required. This is especially important in patients with significant organ dysfunction who are more prone to the effects of local-anaesthetic systemic toxicity." }, { "heading": "Coverage of posterior femoral cutaneous nerve", "text": "The subgluteal approach to the sciatic nerve may not cover posterior femoral cutaneous nerve, unlike proximal lumbar plexus blocks, as the deep investing muscular fascia of the thigh impedes the spread of injectate between the SN and PFCN [1]. Hence, patients often need supplemental injection or systemic analgesia if posterior femoral cutaneous nerve is not blocked. Early supplementation with local anaesthetic should be considered if the posterior thigh block appears to be patchy." } ], "references": [ "Johnson CS, Johnson RL, Niesen AD, Stoike DE, Pawlina W. Ultrasound-Guided Posterior Femoral Cutaneous Nerve Block: A Cadaveric Study. J Ultrasound Med. 2018;37(4):897-903." ] }, { "headingId": "vascular", "subId": null, "initials": "PL", "diagnosis": "Femoral embolectomy", "title": "Intraoperative Hyperkalaemia", "summary": "Patient was a 73-year-old lady listed for a priority 1 femoral embolectomy for acute limb ischaemia in the emergency theatre. She had end-stage renal failure currently not on dialysis but planned for initiation haemodialysis (through newly created left radiocephalic arteriovenous fistula). Complications included hyperkalaemia K 5.2 with no electrocardiography changes for which the primary team had given Resonium, and acidosis on bicarbonate. A venous blood gas preoperatively noted K to be still elevated at 5.1. Rapid sequence induction (RSI) was performed with rocuronium (Suxamethonium was avoided due to the risk of exacerbating hyperkalaemia), and surgery proceeded. Post-induction, insulin and dextrose 50% were given due to the expected rise in potassium after reperfusion. Repeat arterial blood gas in 30minutes showed normalisation of potassium to 4.4. Intraoperatively, judicious fluids were given, and normal saline was chosen as it was potassium-free. 1 pint of blood was transfused due to blood losses. Post-transfusion, a repeat arterial blood gas showed a potassium of 5.4, and another dose of insulin and dextrose 50% was given. At the end of surgery, neuromuscular reversal was done with sugammadex and confirmed with a nerve stimulator before extubation. The patient was sent to the high dependency postoperatively.", "points": [ { "heading": "Rapid sequence induction (RSI) in end-stage renal failure (ESRF)", "text": "Patients with ESRF often have hyperkalaemia, which may contraindicate the use of suxamethonium, and require rocuronium for RSI. However, about 30% rocuronium is excreted renally, and duration of action is prolonged in renal impairment. Sugammadex can provide rapid and complete antagonism of deep neuromuscular bock, but usage in renal impairment is also controversial, as the sugammadex-rocuronium complex may remain in circulation for longer. Therefore, neuromuscular monitoring is especially important before and after reversal." }, { "heading": "Intraoperative hyperkalaemia during reperfusion", "text": "Acute limb ischaemia carries a risk of hyperkalaemia during reperfusion after embolectomy. Frequent monitoring with arterial blood gas allows for early detection and management of hyperkalaemia. This is especially important patients with other risk factors for hyperkalaemia, such as end-stage renal failure and blood transfusion. Intravenous insulin with dextrose 50% is the standard management of hyperkalaemia, but in patients with electrocardiogram changes, intravenous calcium gluconate also stabilises the cardiac membrane. Although the onset of intravenous insulin is about 15-30min, potassium and glucose should be checked about 1hr after administration to determine if further boluses are required." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "regional", "subId": null, "initials": "SB", "diagnosis": "Popliteal sciatic block for right 1st toe disarticulation", "title": "Failed Peripheral Nerve Block", "summary": "Patient was an 86-year-old gentleman, 56kg in weight with a body mass index of 20, who presented with pain and swelling over his right 1st toe. Past medical history was significant for diabetes, hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, ischaemic heart disease on aspirin. He had a previous right lower limb angioplasty under moderate anaesthesia care which he tolerated well. He was planned for a right 1st toe disarticulation in the emergency theatre under regional anaesthesia. Intraoperatively, we decided to perform a popliteal-sciatic and saphenous nerve block. It was one of the first few times my junior was performing a block, so I had to guide her heavily for the block. Thankfully there were not many cases on the emergency board and the other theatre was stable. I went through the steps of the block and the key points to take note off before we brought the patient in. We did a pre-scan which showed good ultrasound anatomy, then proceeded to do the popliteal-sciatic nerve block with ultrasound guidance. I scrubbed in with her and made sure her needle tip was well visualised before advancing. I also aimed to place the local anaesthetic outside the sheath as I did not want to risk needling the nerve. Upon sensory testing with an ice block, the patient still reported patchy areas of cold sensation on the surgical site. We waited for another 10 minutes, after which the sensory testing reported loss of sensation to ice, following which the patient was cleaned and draped. On knife to skin, the patient still moved and complained of pain. We decided to give 25 + 25 mcg of fentanyl for the pain, which resolved his pain. However, as he was still fidgeting, we decided to sedate him with a propofol infusion, which calmed him down. The rest of the surgery proceeded uneventfully. The next day, the acute pain service junior saw the patient and reported complete resolution of numbness.", "points": [ { "heading": "Challenges in supervising a junior performing a block", "text": "Guiding an inexperienced junior through a block was much more difficult than I expected, and I had to be very specific and detailed in my instructions to ensure that they are appropriately carried out. Pre-scanning was useful in ensuring an easy anatomy for the block. I was also more conservative in the proximity of the needle to the nerve as the needle visualisation was difficult and I did not want to risk inadvertent intraneural injection. In retrospect, I could have taken over earlier to position the needle at the appropriate position before injection local anaesthetic. It is also important to balance patient safety with junior education. Patient selection is important especially if the patient had been at high risk for general anaesthesia and the block had to succeed. A busy emergency theatre or time-sensitive surgery may also be less ideal for teaching. However, on-call is where many junior have the most exposure to bread-and-butter blocks such as popliteal block due to the nature of the case." }, { "heading": "Options for failed or patchy block", "text": "It is always important to check the performance of a block with an ice block after injection, even in the presence of good spread on ultrasound, as it provides a window of opportunity to either supplement or repeating the block before the surgery has started. Other options include systemic analgesia such as fentanyl. Available options should always be discussed with the patient, with prompt conversion to general anaesthetic if the patient is keen." }, { "heading": "Differentiating pain from anxiety", "text": "If the patient moves intraoperatively, other causes such as anxiety should be considered. Sedation should be done cautiously in elderly patients at risk of respiratory depression or haemodynamic instability, and over-sedation can also paradoxically lead to more movement." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "regional", "subId": null, "initials": "HC", "diagnosis": "Popliteal sciatic block for right 2nd toe ray amputation", "title": "Residual Neurological Deficit After Peripheral Nerve Block", "summary": "Patient was a 68-year-old gentleman, 94kg in weight with a body mass index of 32, who presented with right 2nd toe wet gangrene on a background of severe peripheral arterial disease. Significant past medical history included poorly controlled diabetes, hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, and ischaemic heart disease complicated by heart failure with preserved ejection fraction. He was planned for a right 2nd toe ray amputation. Preoperatively, reduced sensation was noted in bilateral lower limbs in a gloves and stocking distribution, about 50% of normal, but was able to move toes. Intraoperatively, a right popliteal-sciatic nerve block was performed for surgical anaesthesia. The anatomy was difficult due to large body habitus, but 20ml of 0.5% ropivacaine was infiltrated perineurally with good spread noted. The block was tested after and was successful, and the surgery completed uneventfully. On postoperative day (POD) 1, on acute pain service (APS) review, patient had persistent numbness of his right up to his ankle, associated with toe weakness. On POD2, numbness improved but was still not at baseline, and he still had toe weakness. On POD3, numbness improved to baseline, although patient still had toe weakness that he felt was not at baseline. On POD5, patient still felt that his toe weakness was still not at baseline, and was offered neurology referral in view of persistent motor block. Neurology requested for a nerve conduction study (NCS), which showed diffusely reduced sensory and motor responses consistent with severe sensorimotor axonal peripheral neuropathy. Neurology impression was diabetic neuropathy. On POD12, motor block had completely resolved, and patient was discharged from APS follow-up.", "points": [ { "heading": "Management of residual neurological deficits after peripheral nerve block", "text": "Peripheral nerve blocks may take 1-2 days for complete resolution. However, up to 10% of patients may have transient neurological deficits for days to weeks after a block. In these cases, it is important to rule out serious complications such as a worsening haematoma, ischaemia or severe nerve injury. Progressive deficits, motor weakness and worsening pain may hence require an urgent neurological consultation. In the absence of red flags, patients may be reassured that majority of residual deficits are neuropraxia and 95% resolve in 6 weeks." }, { "heading": "Peripheral neve block in patients with pre-existing peripheral neuropathy", "text": "Patients with pre-existing peripheral neuropathy are at higher risk of prolonged neurological deficits and neurotoxicity, which may be due to a double crush syndrome. While not strictly a contraindication to peripheral nerve block, patients should be counselled appropriately and risk and benefits of peripheral nerve block should be considered, and preoperative neurology documented. A lower concentration of local anaesthetic may be required, and may also be less neurotoxic." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "spine", "subId": null, "initials": "GV", "diagnosis": "T10-L2 posterior decompression and instrumentation", "title": "Thoracolumbar Decompression In A Patient With Metastatic Pathological Fracture", "summary": "This was a 72-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer with bony metastasis. He presented with bilateral lower limb weakness (power 4/5) and was found to have a pathological fracture at T12 causing severe spinal canal compression and cord compression. He was listed as a Priority 2 case for posterior decompression and instrumentation from T10 to L2 in the emergency theatre. Preoperative haemoglobin was 9.2 g/dL. After discussion with surgeons, decision was made not for cell saver. Post-induction, arterial line and large-bore intravenous access was obtained, and fluids were given through a Hotline fluid warmer. Additional active warming was provided by a forced air warmer and by keeping operating theatre temperature at 25 degrees until patient was draped. Tranexamic acid bolus was given followed by infusion. Albumin 5% was started after 1 litre of crystalloids. After 3 units of packed cells were given, a point of care viscoelastic array test was done (ROTEM), and 2g of fibrinogen was given. In total, blood loss was about 1.5L. The patient required phenylephrine infusion which was weaned off by the end of surgery, and was transferred to the high-dependency unit postoperatively for haemodynamic monitoring.", "points": [ { "heading": "High bleeding risk in metastatic spine surgery", "text": "Decompression and instrumentation of metastatic disease is associated with high bleeding risk as tumours are often vascular with fragile and friable vessels. The risk of bleeding increases with a greater number of levels. Additionally, systemic malignancy and chemotherapeutics may be interfere with coagulation, and there should be a low threshold for point-of-care testing with viscoelastic testing early during blood loss." }, { "heading": "Cell-saver in malignancy", "text": "The use of cell-saver is contraindicated in oncological surgeries, as salvaged blood from the surgical field may contain tumour cells that may be transfused and distributed throughout the body and potentially increase the risk of further metastasis. However, these risks are mainly theoretical and has not been well studied. A recent meta-analysis noted that there has been no case report of cancer spread due to cell-saver. Risk also decreases with the use of leukocyte reduction filters, as well as with certain cancer types (e.g. This theoretical risk also needs to be balanced with the proven benefits of cell-saver use in high bleeding risk surgeries and the risks of transfusion. As clinical equipoise exists between these two options, cell-saver may still be useful, and multidisciplinary patient-centric decision should be reached." } ], "references": [ "Frietsch T, Steinbicker AU, Horn A, et al. Safety of Intraoperative Cell Salvage in Cancer Surgery: An Updated Meta-Analysis of the Current Literature. Transfus Med Hemother. 2022;49(3.:143-157. Published 2022 May 11." ] }, { "headingId": "spine", "subId": null, "initials": "XR", "diagnosis": "Posterior lumbar decompression and fusion at L3/L4 and L4/L5, keep in view L2/L3", "title": "Spine Surgery With Intraoperative Neuromonitoring", "summary": "This was a 77-year-old man (59 kg, 156 cm) with spinal stenosis undergoing posterior lumbar decompression and fusion at L3/L4 and L4/L5, keep in view L2/L3. His medical history included hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, and smoking. Baseline haemoglobin was 12.1. Intraoperative neuromonitoring (IONM) was done using somatosensory evoked potentials (SSEPs) and motor evoked potentials (MEPs). Anaesthesia was maintained with total intravenous anaesthesia (TIVA) using propofol and remifentanil, and no neuromuscular blockade was used after initial atracurium used for intubation, in view of IOMN. During dissection, irritation the right L4 nerve root was picked up by the neurophysiologist, and the surgical team was alerted immediately. After decompression, improvement in signals were noted. Throughout this period, a stable anaesthesia milieu was maintained. Postoperatively, the patient could move both his lower limbs with no new neurological deficits.", "points": [ { "heading": "Anaesthesia during usage of IOMN", "text": "A major anaesthetic consideration is to avoid interference with IOMN. Most anaesthetic drugs affect evoked potentials to varying degrees. Additionally, physiological changes such as hypotension, hypocapnia, hypoxia, anaemia and hypothermia can all affect signals. A stable anaesthetic and physiological environment is preferred to allow for accurate interpretation of signal changes. Additionally, a bite block is required during motor evoked potentials." }, { "heading": "Management of signal changes", "text": "In the event of signal changes, it is important to check exactly which signal is affected. The anaesthetic chart should be reviewed to evaluate for any recent changes. An arterial blood gas is useful to check for anaemia and hypocarbia. Temporarily increasing mean arterial pressure with push-dose pressors may be helpful. Close communication with the neurophysiologist and surgeons is essential, as the surgeons may require readjustment of implants, or aborting the procedure in severe cases." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "eye", "subId": null, "initials": "BT", "diagnosis": "Right eye phacoemulsification for cataract", "title": "Sedation For Cataract Surgery", "summary": "1st Case\nPatient was a 71-year-old woman who was ASA 3 for ischaemic heart disease (EF 55%), atrial fibrillation on apixaban, diabetes mellitus, and cervical myelopathy. She was planned for Right eye phacoemulsification for cataract under monitored anaesthetic care. After cleaning and draping, 10+10+10mg of propofol was given. However, she continued to move, despite repeated attempts by surgeon to tell her not to move. She was able to shake her head when asked if there was any pain. As the initial propofol boluses were ineffective, we reassured her and held her hand throughout the procedure, which calmed her down and helped her tolerate the rest of the surgery without moving.\n\n2nd Case\nPatient was a 63-year-old gentleman who was ASA 3 for poorly controlled diabetes, hypertension, and hyperlipidaemia. He was planned for Left eye phacoemulsification and lens implantation, vitrectomy, endolaser under monitored anaesthetic care. The surgery was estimated to be at least an hour long, and the surgeons planned to give a peribulbar block. As the surgery was likely to be long, decision was made for TCI propofol. We started the propofol at an effect site concentration (Cet) of 0.5mcg/ml, which helped the patient tolerate the block together with alfentanil. However, patient started moving at knife to skin. Propofol was increased to Cet 0.7mcg/ml, but patient continued to move. The surgeon noted patient was snoring and requested to reduce the sedation, and propofol was reduced to 0.3mcg/ml. Patients hand was held, and after a brief surgical pause, patient stopped moving and was able to nod to commands. The rest of surgery proceeded uneventfully on propofol 0.3mcg/ml.", "points": [ { "heading": "Paradoxical agitation after propofol", "text": "Paradoxical agitation from benzodiazepines such as midazolam are well described, but may also be seen after propofol and other sedatives. Additionally, certain surgeries require a still patient, which may be better achieved with lighter sedation and a cooperative patient. It is hence important to assess if the patient is still responsive and cooperative before deepening sedation, and if the patient is already not responsive, the appropriate action may be to lighten sedation instead. For longer procedures, it may be easier to use TCI propofol for more precise titration of sedation." }, { "heading": "Nonpharmacological methods of anxiolysis", "text": "Movement may be due to pain or other discomfort rather than anxiety, and this should be assessed and local anaesthetic topped up if needed. Additionally, nonpharmacological methods of anxiolysis such as verbal reassurance and physical touch can often be more useful. Good preoperative counselling on what to expect also goes a long way to helping patients stay calm." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "eye", "subId": null, "initials": "NY", "diagnosis": "Penetrating eye injury", "title": "Penetrating Eye Injury With Open Globe", "summary": "This patient was a 32-year-old Bangladeshi male who sustained a penetrating injury to his eye when a metal shard entered his eye during machining. He was planned for priority 1 open globe repair under general anaesthesia in the emergency theatre. Rapid sequence induction was done using propofol, high-dose remifentanil and rocuronium, avoiding suxamethonium, with esmolol on standby during laryngoscopy. The endotracheal tube was taped and not tied. Anaesthesia was maintained with total intravenous anaesthesia (TIVA). Ventilation was titrated to avoid hypercarbia and excessive positive end-expiratory pressure. The surgery proceeded uneventfully. Dual antiemetic prophylaxis with dexamethasone and ondansetron was given. Smooth emergence without coughing and bucking was achieved with remifentanil infusion.", "points": [ { "heading": "Anaesthetic management of the open globe", "text": "The primary goal for an open globe injury is to minimize increases in intraocular pressure, to avoid extrusion of intraocular contents. Physiological factors such as hypercarbia and hypertension increase intraocular arterial blood volume and should be avoided. Good venous drainage should be maintained by head up positioning, avoiding endotracheal tube ties and excessive positive end-expiratory pressure. Coughing and straining should be avoided, and a smooth emergence is preferred. Muscle relaxation is helpful. External pressure on the eye such as with a face mask should be avoided." }, { "heading": "Airway management in open globe", "text": "Rapid sequence induction may be required as these cases are often urgent without adequate time for fasting. Rocuronium should be used as suxamethonium can cause ocular muscle fasciculations and raise IOP. Adequate opioid use helps to blunt the sympathetic response to laryngoscopy and resultant blood pressure increase. If aspiration risk is low, laryngeal mask airway may be preferred as it cause less sympathetic stimulation and coughing on induction and emergence. Emergence may be done on remifentanil aiming for a smooth emergence." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "neuro", "subId": null, "initials": "DQ", "diagnosis": "Transsphenoidal resection of pituitary microadenoma", "title": "Transsphenoidal Resection Of Pituitary Microadenoma In A Patient With Cushing’s Disease", "summary": "This patient was a 37-year-old woman (158 cm, 80 kg, BMI 32. planned for a transsphenoidal resection of pituitary microadenoma. She had a 6.9 mm left pituitary microadenoma, with biochemically confirmed Cushing’s disease (elevated ACTH with normal 8 am cortisol and TFTs). Significant medical history included diabetes mellitus, hyperlipidaemia, fatty liver, and obesity.\nPreoperatively, endocrine advised starting IV hydrocortisone 50 mg Q6H on the day of surgery, and subsequent weaning as per endocrine. The patient was positioned as for sinus surgery, with a slight head elevation, and oral intubation with tube swung to the left and a throat pack. General anaesthesia was induced and maintained with TIVA. An intra-arterial line was inserted, and blood pressure kept controlled with remifentanil. Patient was extubated smoothly on remifentanil to avoid bucking.", "points": [ { "heading": "Anaesthetic considerations for pituitary surgery", "text": "Preoperative evaluation focuses on whether the tumour is functional or not, followed by subsequent endocrine optimisation. Large tumours may also cause mass effect and visual field defects. Intraoperatively, the goal is to minimize bleeding and optimise the surgical field via tight blood pressure control and avoiding hypertension. However, hypotension is also avoided which can compromise cerebral perfusion pressure. Like in sinus surgery, a south Ring–Adair–Elwyn (RAE) tube is inserted and swung to the left, and a throat pack is inserted. Normocarbia is preferred as hypocarbia can pull the tumour upwards and reduce accessibility of the tumour. Smooth emergence is important to avoid increases in intracranial pressure which may lead to postoperative cerebrospinal fluid leaks." }, { "heading": "Steroids in transsphenoidal surgery", "text": "Steroid management depends on the patient’s hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. In patients with intact HPA axis, avoid giving steroids intraoperatively to allow for accurate postoperative evaluation of cortisol levels. Patients with Cushing’s disease due to an ACTH-secreting tumour, however, require perioperative supplementation of steroids to avoid adrenal crisis. Endocrine consultation perioperatively is critical." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "neuro", "subId": null, "initials": "LW", "diagnosis": "Clipping of left MCA aneurysm", "title": "Clipping of Intracranial Aneurysm", "summary": "This was a 63yearold lady admitted with left subarachnoid haemorrhage (WFNS grade 1. due to a left middle cerebral artery (MCA) aneurysm. Significant medical history included hypertension, chronic kidney disease, and glaucoma. She was admitted to the neurosurgical high dependency, then sent for a 4-vessel angiography in which they were unable to coil the aneurysm. She was then listed for surgical clipping of her left MCA aneurysm. She already had an arterial line from the neurosurgical high dependency, and decision was made to insert her central venous catheter post-induction to minimize pain-related spikes in blood pressure. Gentle controlled induction was done with target-controlled infusion (TCI) propofol and high dose remifentanil, with esmolol on standby to control her blood pressure. Anaesthesia was then maintained with propofol and remifentanil, which was titrated up during pinning. After the aneurysm was dissected out, temporary clipping of the M1 segment was performed, and thiopentone was given for cerebral protection. Indocyanine green was then used to confirm occlusion, before the permanent clip was applied. Patient was given fentanyl towards the end of the procedure, and was extubated awake.", "points": [ { "heading": "Anaesthetic management of clipping", "text": "Strict blood pressure control is critical to avoid increases in transmural pressure which may cause aneurysmal rupture or bleeding, especially if there has been a recent bleed. Pre-induction arterial line with good local anaesthesia allows for close monitoring of blood pressure on induction. Central venous access can be done post-induction to hypertension due to pain. Remifentanil and esmolol are useful to blunt the sympathetic response to laryngoscopy, intubation and pinning. Hypotension should be avoided to maintain cerebral perfusion pressure. Preparation should also be made for potentially large amounts of blood loss. Large bore intravenous access is essential, with blood on standby." }, { "heading": "Neuroprotection during temporary clipping", "text": "Temporary clipping of a feeding vessel is often required to allow for aneurysm dissection and control. During these period, cerebral blood supply is compromised. Thiopentone bolus can reduce cerebral metabolic rate and provide some neuroprotection. Indocyanine green may also be requested for angiography to confirm successful clipping." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "challenging", "subId": null, "initials": "MZ", "diagnosis": "Surgical fixation of left humerus fracture", "title": "Surgical Fixation of Left Humerus Fracture In A Pregnant Patient At 33 Weeks Gestation", "summary": "This was a 35-year-old woman admitted for surgical fixation of a left humerus fracture after a mechanical fall. Importantly, she was also 33 weeks pregnant with a viable intrauterine pregnancy. The pregnancy had otherwise been uneventful antenatally with no complications. Other medical history included hypothyroidism on thyroxine and multiple drug allergies (paracetamol, NSAIDs, penicillin). Since admission, she was started on prophylactic Clexane. She also had a supraclavicular block catheter inserted by acute pain service which was patchy, and also had tramadol and oxycodone for analgesia. Preoperatively, patient was counselled on the risks of surgery with a third trimester pregnancy. A baseline foetal heart rate was checked in the ward. Intraoperatively, rapid sequence induction was performed. The catheter was bolused and taped away from the surgical site. An abdominal lead gown was used to shield the foetus. Excessive abdominal flexion during beach chair positioning was avoided. Parecoxib and dexamethasone were avoided due to potential foetal effects. Atropine was used instead of glycopyrrolate for reversal to avoid foetal bradycardia. Postoperatively, foetal heart beat was assessed with doppler ultrasound in the recovery area by a delivery suite nurse.", "points": [ { "heading": "Management of foetal risk", "text": "Nonurgent surgery should ideally be postponed until after pregnancy, although multidisciplinary discussion with involvement of the obstetrician is important. The anaesthetic goals are to avoid pregnancy loss due to premature labour, maintain foetal wellbeing, and avoid teratogenicity. Locoregional techniques are preferred as it minimizes foetal drug exposure. Uteroplacental perfusion should be strictly maintained, and foetal hypoxemia and acidosis avoided. NSAIDs should be avoided in 1st and 3rd trimester. Increases in intra-abdominal pressure should be minimized. Atropine should be used instead of glycopyrrolate to avoid foetal bradycardia with neostigmine. Radiation should be avoided or minimized, and the patient’s abdomen should be shielded if required. Foetal heart rate and uterine activity should be monitored perioperatively." }, { "heading": "Management of maternal physiological changes", "text": "Parturients in their third semester are at higher risk of difficult airway and aspiration, with a reduced safe apnoea time. Regional anaesthesia is preferred, and if general anaesthesia is required, airway management should take into account the above risks (e.g. rapid sequence induction).  Aortocaval compression can also lead to reduced cardiac output and hypotension, and left lateral positioning is ideal if the surgery allows." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "challenging", "subId": null, "initials": "CE", "diagnosis": "Left ankle free flap reconstruction", "title": "Left Ankle Free Flap Reconstruction With Popliteal Catheter", "summary": "This was a 67-year-old man (56 kg, 162 cm) who was planned for a left ankle free flap reconstruction using a right anterolateral thigh donor site. He had a nonhealing left ankle wound after resection of a lateral ankle squamous cell carcinoma. He was otherwise ASA 3 for hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, paroxysmal atrial fibrillation on clopidogrel, recurrent ischaemic strokes, and SIADH, but he had undergone multiple previous inpatient procedures under both peripheral nerve block and general anaesthesia which were generally uneventful without vasopressors. Preoperative haemoglobin was 11 g/dL. Intraoperatively, a popliteal catheter was inserted pre-induction. An out-of-plane technique was used. Saline was initially used for hydrodissection, and local anaesthetic was bolused through the catheter after insertion, and tested with ice. An intra-arterial line was placed for blood pressure monitoring and arterial blood gas. Phenylephrine boluses were required initially during catheter insertion, but no longer required for the rest of the procedure. Mean arterial pressure was kept above 65, and haemoglobin was kept about 8. Blood loss was replaced with albumin after the first litre of crystalloids to avoid flap oedema. The procedure took approximately 8 hours, and was uneventful.", "points": [ { "heading": "Free flap considerations", "text": "Anaesthetic management in free flap surgery is centred around maintaining oxygen delivery to the flap. Adequate mean arterial pressure and fluid resuscitation is important to maintain perfusion. However, fluid overload should also be provided as it can lead to excessive flap oedema. Anaemia and hypoxemia should also be avoided. Vasoconstriction should also be avoided, and use of vasopressors minimized through careful haemodynamic management. Hypothermia-induced vasoconstriction should be avoided through aggressive active warming, and hypercapnia avoided. Good analgesia reduces pain-induced sympathetic stimulation, as well as aggressive antiemesis. An arterial line allows for regular arterial blood gas monitoring, and may also minimize pressure-related injuries from prolonged non-invasive blood pressure cuff inflation." }, { "heading": "Popliteal catheter insertion", "text": "Regional anaesthesia and catheter insertion can be especially beneficial for microsurgery as it helps promote vasodilation. Popliteal catheters provide excellent postoperative analgesia for surgery below the knee. While an in-plane approach can be done, an out-of-plane approach follows the course of the nerve and may help to keep a longer portion of the catheter in contact with the nerve." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "lscs", "subId": null, "initials": "VA", "diagnosis": "Caesarean section", "title": "Patchy Epidural Top-Up For Caesarean Section", "summary": "Patient was a 33-year-old lady, primigravida, for emergency lower segment caesarean section (emLSCS) for failure to progress. She already had a labour epidural that was inserted 8 hours ago, and on testing there was bilateral loss of sensation till T11. Decision was made to top up the epidural for surgical anaesthesia. On transfer to the operating theatre, 10 mL of 2% lignocaine with adrenaline and bicarbonate was given in 5ml aliquots. After 15 minutes, the block was still only at T8. She was placed head down and another 5ml of epidural cocktail was given, but block height was still at T6. In the meantime, dop-tone check of the baby’s heart rate was normal. As block level was improving, decision was made to top up another 5ml of epidural cocktail and prep the abdomen for surgery. On skin testing, dermatome level was at T4 to ice bock, and could not feel pinching at the surgical incision site.\n\nHowever, on incision and mobilisation of deeper layers, patient felt significant distress, either from a patchy block or anxiety. After discussion with patient and surgeon, decision was made to proceed with caesarean section and nitrous oxide was provided for the patient for analgesia. Conversion to general anaesthesia was offered to the patient but she was not keen. Soon after, baby was delivered. After delivery, 25mcg of fentanyl was given and maternal discomfort improved significantly. Initial skin-to-skin was done and rest of LSCS proceeded uneventfully.", "points": [ { "heading": "Assessment of epidural top-up", "text": "It is essential to check if the epidural has been working well before epidural top-up, and to thoroughly assess block height and quality before and after an epidural top-up. If there is doubts on whether the epidural is properly working, can consider adjusting the catheter, doing a spinal anaesthesia or repeating the combined-spinal epidural. All of these have their own drawbacks. General anaesthesia is also a consideration if urgent delivery is required." }, { "heading": "Communication with surgeons and patient", "text": "Clear communication with patient and surgeons is essential throughout this process. Patients may be keen for conversion to general anaesthesia earlier, as pain during caesarean section can be extremely distressing." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "lscs", "subId": null, "initials": "OI", "diagnosis": "Caesarean section", "title": "Postpartum Haemorrhage (PPH) During Caesarean Section After Prolonged Labour", "summary": "Patient was a 35-year-old lady, primigravida, planned for a lower segment caesarean section (LSCS) for failure to progress after 12 hours despite oxytocin. Epidural top-up was performed, achieving a T4 block with 10ml of cocktail. Delivery of the baby was uneventful. After placental delivery, significant uterine bleeding was noted despite Carbetocin and Oxytocin infusion.\n\nWhile the obstetric team continued uterine massage, tranexamic acid (TXA) 1g was administered. Intravenous 250mcg ergometrine was given slowly with another 250mcg in the drip, followed by intramuscular Carboprost 5 minutes later. After 15 minutes, Carboprost was redosed as advised by the surgeons. At this point, total blood loss was estimated at 1 litre with a baseline haemoglobin of 10.1. Gelafundin was started. ROTEM was held off as uterine tone had improved and bleeding had slowed. Another 1g of TXA was given after 30 minutes. Haemodynamics remained relatively stable with fluid resuscitation and phenylephrine boluses. Patient was sent to delivery suite postoperatively for closer monitoring.", "points": [ { "heading": "PPH risk factors and anticipation", "text": "Prolonged labour and oxytocin use are major risk factors for uterine atony and intraoperative PPH, and should be anticipated. Other risk factors include tocolytics and general anaesthesia, obesity, uterine distension such as from macrosomia or twin pregnancies, abnormal placentation, and chorioamnionitis. The other 4Ts should also be considered (Trauma, Tissue, Thrombin)." }, { "heading": "Stepwise management of intraoperative PPH", "text": "Management of intraoperative PPH requires prompt and timely steps and close coordination between obstetric and anaesthetic teams. Uterotonics and TXA should be given early and re-dosed as appropriate (note contraindications for example pre-eclampsia and asthma). Early ROTEM and fibrinogen are also important. Haemodynamically, it is important to consider use of colloids as well as activation of blood transfusion protocols, as well as large bore intravenous access and fluid warming. Surgical options and interventional radiology should be considered early if bleeding persists. Conversion to general anaesthesia may become necessary if patient becomes increasingly unstable or surgical duration is prolonged." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "labour", "subId": null, "initials": "TF", "diagnosis": "Labour analgesia", "title": "Difficult Epidural In A High Body Mass Index Patient", "summary": "Patient was a 34-year-old G2P1 lady with a body mass index (BMI of 44 and 116 kg in weight. She was 5cm dilated and in significant distress, keen for epidural. On review of her notes, it was noted that her previous epidural took 3 attempts. Given the anticipated difficulty, neuraxial ultrasound was used to identify the midline, interspinous space, and estimate the depth to the epidural space, after she was positioned. The L3/4 space was marked out, and the depth was estimated at approximately 6cm. She was then cleaned and draped. Loss of resistance was successfully achieved at 7cm, followed by a combined spinal-epidural technique. The patient reported immediate relief of contraction pain and continued to have satisfactory pain relief for the duration of her labour.", "points": [ { "heading": "Epidural in high BMI patients", "text": "Epidurals in such patients are difficult due to difficulty in palpating landmarks and increased depth to epidural space. Having appropriate equipment such as ultrasound on standby, as well as informing a senior anaesthetist, can be helpful. Early placement of epidurals while patients are not in distress can help to optimise epidural placement conditions." }, { "heading": "Use of ultrasound in epidural placement", "text": "Ultrasound can help to identify the midline and interspace, and estimate the depth to the epidural space. This can be done preprocedurally before cleaning and draping. However, it is important to ask the patient not to move, otherwise skin markings might shift. In patients who are unable to remain still, performing the ultrasonography while sterile may allow for repeated ultrasonography in the event of patient movement." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "labour", "subId": null, "initials": "EJ", "diagnosis": "Labour analgesia", "title": "Breakthrough Pain And One-Sided Epidural", "summary": "Patient was a 32-year-old G1P0 lady at 5 cm cervical dilation for epidural analgesia. A combined spinal-epidural was performed. The epidural was inserted at L3/4 using a midline approach with loss of resistance to saline at 5 cm, following which a catheter was secured to 10 cm at the skin. Test dose was negative, and initial level was at T10. Epidural continuous infusion was started at 10ml/hr. 2 hours after epidural infusion initiation, the patient complained of worsening contraction pain. On assessment, patient had predominantly abdominal pain, and sensory level was at L1. Lignocaine bolus 5ml 1% was given and infusion was increased to 14ml/hr from 12ml/hr. 15 minutes later, sensory level had improved only to T12 and patient was still complaining of abdominal pain. Another 5ml of lignocaine 1% was given with fentanyl 50mcg epidurally, but the level remained at T12 after 10 minutes. The consultant epiduralist was informed, and the epidural pulled out by 1cm to 9cm at skin, and another 5ml of lignocaine 1% was given. Despite this, the epidural level remained at T12 with minimal improvement to patient’s pain. After discussion with the patient, the decision was made to resite the epidural. A second combined spinal epidural was performed at L4/5, which provided good analgesia for the remainder of the labour.", "points": [ { "heading": "Troubleshooting poorly working epidural", "text": "Around 10% of patients get inadequate analgesia on epidural. There are many reasons for breakthrough pain after an epidural, including inadequate or patchy block, catheter migration or malplacement, sacral sparing, or occiput-posterior positioning, among other causes. Hence, it is critical to take a history and examine the patient and the catheter to determine the likely causes. If the block is bilateral but patchy or inadequately dense, administering a higher volume or concentration, addition of fentanyl or withdrawing the catheter slightly can help." }, { "heading": "Importance of early reassessment and escalation to senior", "text": "Persistent pain despite troubleshooting should prompt frequent reassessment. Delays in analgesia optimisation and resiting a poorly working epidural can heavily impact a patient’s experience. However, the patient should be communicated to throughout the entire troubleshooting process so that they can make their preferences known, rather than assuming their preferences. A senior anaesthetist should be informed if an epidural persistently does not work well, for consideration of resite." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "paeds", "subId": null, "initials": "IK", "diagnosis": "Oral rehabilitation", "title": "Preoperative Counselling For High-Risk Surgery For Congenital Muscular Diseases", "summary": "The first patient was a 6-year-old boy (24 kg, BMI 15) with SMA Type 2 and severe motor delay. He was wheelchair-bound with limb contractures, and was on gene therapy (Risdiplam). He also had severe obstructive sleep apnoea, on nocturnal BiPAP (OAHI 24/hr, nadir SpO₂ 88%). He had 2 previous respiratory collapses when he had an upper respiratory tract infection in 2019, which was then complicated by possible hypoxic ischaemic encephalopathy and developmental delay. Mentally, he only could say single words. He did not have any cardiac issues, and was cleared by cardiology. Lastly, he also had features of difficult airway, with buck teeth and receding chin. He was listed for oral rehabilitation with multiple dental extractions (2-hour case). He had already been seen and counselled by the junior in the anaesthesia clinic, but due to the high risks, my consultant and myself decided to speak to the parents over the phone the day before surgery. The father was aware that he was quoted “high risks”, but there was some disconnect in terms of the implications. He did not know that the child will definitely be going to high dependency postoperatively, and did not know there was a risk of inability to extubate and requiring intensive care subsequently. He did not know that a respiratory collapse with similar consequences to his previous collapse might recur perioperatively. After discussing with each other, they decided to cancel the surgery.\n\nThe second patient was a 28yearold gentleman with Duchenne muscular dystrophy. He was wheelchairbound and tubefed via nasogastric tube. He required nasal CPAP support for most of the day, and can only remove the CPAP for a few hours each day. Unfortunately, he had been having recurrent aspirations over the past few years, which had become more frequent. He was currently admitted for a pneumonia. The paediatric surgeons planned to do a percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) tube insertion under monitored anaesthesia care (MAC) to reduce his aspiration risks. They also required an oesophagoduodenoscopy (OGD) to guide the PEG into place. I was referred as an anaesthesia blue letter to do a preoperative review of the patient. During counselling, I noted that the patient was unaware and that there was a relatively high risk of conversion to general anaesthesia, especially if the CPAP could not be applied during the OGD or be unable to function due to leak. If we have to intubate him, there was a chance his pulmonary function could decompensate, and might require prolonged ventilation and intensive care. In addition, there’s a risk of potential hypoxic collapse. After further discussion with the primary team, the patient decided not to proceed with surgery.", "points": [ { "heading": "Importance of contextualized preoperative counselling", "text": "An important aim of preoperative counselling for high-risk patients is to help patients understand the risks in terms of perioperative implications, rather than simply labelling as high risk. While these patients may have been counselled by the surgeons in terms of surgical complications, anaesthetic-specific risks and general perioperative complications will need to be discussed. The goal is not to scare patients, but to provide a realistic view that ensures that the patient’s autonomy is maximized and the management is in line with their values. Specific areas that may need to be discussed include the inability to extubate and potential need for prolonged postoperative ventilation, or the implications of a hypoxic event." }, { "heading": "Assessment of congenital muscular diseases", "text": "Patients with congenital muscular diseases are at high risk for perioperative respiratory decompensation, and a key goal in the assessment is to establish their respiratory reserves (effort tolerance, dependence on BiPAP, arterial blood gas and spirometry results). They are also often associated with bulbar dysfunction, obstructive sleep apnoea, restrictive lung disease, spinal deformities and difficult airway. Muscular diseases that affect the heart can also cause cardiomyopathy, and depending on the underlying disorder there may be other comorbidities." }, { "heading": "Anaesthesia for congenital muscular diseases", "text": "Regional anaesthesia is often preferable to avoid the respiratory effects of general anaesthesia. Succinylcholine and volatiles should be avoided, and non-depolarizing neuromuscular blockers only used when needed due to increased sensitivity. Sugammadex is also preferred over neostigmine. Every effort should be done to minimize muscle weakness, such as correcting electrolyte disturbances and avoiding steroids. Multimodal analgesia is preferred to avoid respiratory-depressant effects of opioids. Temperature monitoring is mandatory as they are at high risk of hypothermia, and certain disorders may predispose to malignant hyperthermia. Good suctioning, recruitment, and extubation onto BiPAP can help maximize their success of extubation." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "paeds", "subId": null, "initials": "AS", "diagnosis": "Bilateral herniotomy", "title": "Caudal Anaesthesia For Bilateral Inguinal Herniotomy", "summary": "This was a 5-year-old boy (16kg) was planned for bilateral inguinal herniotomy. He was otherwise well with no medical history. After inhalational induction, we decided to perform a single-shot caudal injection for intra- and postoperative analgesia. The patient was positioned in left lateral in a knee-chest position for the block. On palpation, he had significant subcutaneous tissue and the sacral hiatus was poorly palpated. A 22G cannula was inserted through the sacrococcygeal ligament, and pushed off. However, on injection of saline, significant resistance was felt. The consultant took over and repalpated a different location for the sacral hiatus. This time, the cannula was threaded smoothly and the saline bolus felt like blousing through good intravenous line. 15ml 0f 0.2% ropivacaine was given with a 10ml syringe (as 20ml syringes have different resistance)., and the patient had an uneventful recovery with good postoperative pain control.", "points": [ { "heading": "Caudal anaesthesia", "text": "Caudal anaesthesia is commonly used in young children (less than 20kg), especially for procedures below the umbilicus, and can even be used as the primary anaesthetic in neonates and young infants with sedation. The primary goal is to palpate and identify the sacral hiatus, which forms an equilateral triangle with the posterior superior iliac spines. The Armitage formula can be used as a guide for the volume required, typically dilute local anaesthetics, and additives such as dexmedetomidine 1mcg/kg may be added to prolong duration of action. If the landmarks are poorly palpated, ultrasound can be used for more accurate identification (finding the “frog eyes”). If the pop of the sacrococcygeal ligament is not convincing, saline or air can be used to confirm placement. In older children >20kg, cranial spread is more difficult due to changes in the epidural space, hence caudals are less commonly done." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "remote", "subId": null, "initials": "RN", "diagnosis": "Embolization of dural arteriovenous fistula", "title": "Embolization Of Dural Arteriovenous Fistula (DAVF)", "summary": "This was a 45-year-old woman who was found to have a DAVF along the superior aspect of the left tentorial leaflet on CT angiography, and confirmed to be a Cognard 3 to 4 DAVF on DSA subsequently. She was planned for elective embolization of her DAVF. Her past medical history included asthma (on Seretide), depression, gastritis, and hiatal hernia.\nShe was brought to the interventional radiology suite for elective embolization. An intra-arterial line was inserted pre-induction for tight blood pressure control. General anaesthesia was induced and maintained on total intravenous anaesthesia (TIVA) and remifentanil for blood pressure control. Embolization was completed uneventfully. Smooth emergence was achieved with remifentanil, and she had no neurological deficits postoperatively.", "points": [ { "heading": "Anaesthetic management of DAVF embolization", "text": "Anaesthesia for DAVF involves avoiding rupture by tight control of blood pressure and avoidance of hypertension. Hypotension can also reduce cerebral perfusion pressure and should be avoided. Pre-induction arterial line is important for tight blood pressure control. Increases in intracranial pressure should be avoided, and TIVA with controlled ventilation is preferred. Paralysis should be topped up regularly to avoid movement during critical periods of the surgery. Additionally, the proceduralist may request for periods of temporary hypotension to reduce flow through the fistula to assist in embolization, and remifentanil may be useful. Postoperatively, there is a risk of cerebral hyperperfusion, and close neuromonitoring and blood pressure control with avoidance of hypertension is indicated." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "remote", "subId": null, "initials": "YC", "diagnosis": "Endovascular therapy for terminal internal carotid artery ischaemic stroke", "title": "Anaesthesia for Endovascular Therapy (EVT)", "summary": "This was an 84yearold Chinese female (ASA 3. with multiple comorbidities including HTN, HLD, Atrial fibrillation, and presumed ischaemic heart disease. She was also frail at only 45kg. She presented with drowsiness and right sided weakness, likely from a left sided infarct. She received rTPA and the stroke team was activated. As we were waiting for the nursing staff and proceduralist to arrive, and the patient had significant cardiovascular comorbidities, I decided to get my junior to attempt an intra-arterial (IA) line while I induced the patient and intubated. The arterial line insertion was smooth, and we proceeded to maintain the patient on sevoflurane. Blood pressure was maintained using phenylephrine infusion. Embolectomy was done for a left terminal internal carotid artery clot, and good distal perfusion was noted after. After discussion with the neurologist, the patient was extubated at the end of the case, and was transferred to neuro-medical high dependency.", "points": [ { "heading": "Avoiding delays in EVT", "text": "In EVT, the primary anaesthetic goal is to minimize time from activation to embolectomy, as “time is brain”, and delays in recanalization compromises the ischaemic penumbra. In otherwise well patients, intra-arterial line may be forgone to minimize delay. The proceduralist may also be able to assist in obtaining intra-arterial access in their surgical field. In frailer pati ents, peripheral noradrenaline can serve as an alternative, with a central venous catheter inserted after recanalization if indicated. However, if the patient has poor myocardial function with significant haemodynamic instability preoperatively, rapid insertion of central venous catheter may still be required to allow for inotropic support." }, { "heading": "Haemodynamic targets in EVT", "text": "Blood pressure control in EVT is a balance between maintaining perfusion to the ischaemic penumbra and reducing the risk of haemorrhagic conversion. Before recanalization, SNACC 2014 guidelines recommend SBP 140-180mmHg, and 2026 ASA/AHA guidelines also recommend to keep SBP <180. After recanalization, with an elevated risk of haemorrhagic conversion and hyperperfusion, a target of 120-160mmHg has been suggested. As the procedure is not very stimulating, blood pressure targets can typically be met with a phenylephrine infusion and sufficient depth of anaesthesia. Exact blood pressure targets are best discussed with the neurologist." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "acute", "subId": null, "initials": "HQ", "diagnosis": "Rebound pain after axillary brachial plexus block for surgical fixation of left 3rd and 4th metacarpal fractures and left proximal phalanx fracture.", "title": "Rebound Pain After Regional Anaesthesia", "summary": "Patient was a 47-year-old gentleman, 76kg in weight and 169cm in height. He presented after a road traffic accident, complicated by left 3rd and 4th metacarpal fractures and left proximal phalanx fracture. Additionally, he had right sided 5th to 7th rib fractures with underlying lung contusion but no pneumothorax. Past medical history was significant for diabetes on empagliflozin, hypertension and hyperlipidaemia. He was planned for surgical fixation of left 3rd and 4th metacarpal fractures and left proximal phalanx fracture.\n\nPreoperatively, patient was counselled regional anaesthesia in view of rib fractures and empagliflozin use. An axillary brachial plexus block was performed uneventfully and good sensory and motor blockade was achieved. A supplemental intercostobrachial nerve block was also performed for tourniquet pain. The surgery proceeded uneventfully. Postoperatively he had no pain in the post-operative care unit, and was discharged to the general ward.\n\nIn the general ward, as he was being prepared for discharge on the same day, he complained of mild pain over the surgical site with receding block. Paracetamol and tramadol 50mg were given orally to good effect. He was then sent home. However, he re-presented in the emergency department with surgical site pain, after he developed worsening pain over the surgical site that persisted despite paracetamol, etoricoxib and tramadol. He was reviewed by the surgical team, given 2mg of intravenous morphine with improvement of pain, and educated to continue current analgesia regimen regularly.", "points": [ { "heading": "Incidence and risk factors for rebound pain after regional anaesthesia", "text": "Rebound pain is acute postoperative pain that follows resolution of effective regional anaesthesia, due to unmasking of nociceptive stimuli. It is increasingly recognised as more common than previously thought, in up to 40-50% of patients. It typically occurs after techniques that provide complete analgesia and surgical anaesthesia. Risk factors include bony surgery, preoperative pain or opioid use, young females" }, { "heading": "Prevention of rebound pain after regional anaesthesia", "text": "Despite rebound pain, patients still are more satisfied after a successful regional anaesthesia technique, and would still have it again. The most important step is to provide patient education preoperatively about postoperative pain and the expected course of the resolution of nerve block with inevitable onset of postsurgical pain. Multimodal analgesia should be prescribed and given regularly, and patients should also be counselled on this for ambulatory procedures. Nonpharmacological techniques such as elevation of affected limb are also useful. Finally, in select patients, continuous catheter-based techniques or regional additives such as dexamethasone may be beneficial to extend the duration of analgesia to match the expected pain trajectory." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "acute", "subId": null, "initials": "PW", "diagnosis": "Dexmedetomidine and ketamine infusion for surgical fixation of complex pelvic fractures, open bladder neck repair", "title": "Intraoperative Systemic Multimodal Analgesia", "summary": "Patient was a 40-year-old gentleman with a weight of 78kg and body mass index of 24. He presented after a road traffic accident. His injuries were multiple pelvic fractures with bladder neck injury, L2-5 transverse process fracture, left big toe open dislocation, and multiple abrasions. Past medical history included smoking and gout. He was planned for surgical fixation of his complex pelvic fracture and open bladder neck repair 5 days after his accident. General anaesthesia with endotracheal tube was performed, and propofol and remifentanil used for maintenance. As this was a major surgery listed for 8 hours with high risk of blood loss, arterial line and large bore intravenous access with hotline prepared. I anticipated significant postoperative pain but there were no options for regional anaesthesia to mitigate this. After discussion with my covering consultant, we decided to run a multimodal systemic analgesia strategy with ketamine boluses and dexmedetomidine infusion. Towards the end of the case, he also received a total of 8mg of oxycodone. Ketamine and dexmedetomidine was stopped an hour before the end of the case, and propofol infusion tailed off slowly during skin closure guided by his bispectral index. He was awake and extubated on transfer to the trolley, without excessive delay.", "points": [ { "heading": "Multimodal systemic analgesia", "text": "Pelvic surgery involves major bony work and soft tissue trauma, and is associated with significant postoperative pain. While regional anaesthesia may provide superior analgesia, there are situations where regional anaesthesia is contraindicated or less ideal. In these situations, a combination of nonopioid analgesics and adjuncts can improve analgesic efficiency and reduce opioid requirements. Options include dexmedetomidine, ketamine, magnesium and lignocaine infusions. Regular paracetamol and COX-2 inhibitors should also be given." }, { "heading": "Dexmedetomidine as an analgesic adjunct", "text": "Dexmedetomidine is an alpha-2 agonist that reduces opioid consumption, reduces nausea/vomiting, reduces stress response to surgery, smooths emergence, and may also reduce postoperative delirium. Dexmedetomidine infusion for analgesia can be initiated with or without loading dose, and can also be given as intermittent boluses. Larger doses can result in bradycardia and haemodynamic instability, which can be especially apparent in the postoperative care unit, and lower doses without loading dose should be given to frailer unwell patients. Dexmedetomidine may also prolong emergence, although this can be mitigated by lowering the infusion rate towards the end of surgery." }, { "heading": "Ketamine as an analgesic adjunct", "text": "Ketamine is an NMDA antagonist that can reduce opioid consumption, and is especially useful in patients on long term opioids with chronic pain and central sensitisation. It is also more haemodynamically stable compared to dexmedetomidine. However, it can lead to emergence delirium, nausea, and increased secretions.\nLower, sub-dissociative doses of 0.1-0.3mg/kg/hr for analgesia should be used to minimize side effects." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "icu", "subId": null, "initials": "ZD", "diagnosis": "Urosepsis", "title": "Urosepsis Resuscitation And Early Source Control", "summary": "This was an 82-year-old gentleman who admitted to the ICU for septic shock. He was frail and elderly but had good premorbid function, was ambulant in the community. Computed tomography scan showed right sided hydronephrosis with obstructing ureteric stone, consistent with urosepsis, and venous lactate of 6.4. He was hypotensive despite 1.5 litres of crystalloids in the emergency department, and was started on peripheral noradrenaline. The urology team was initially keen to stabilise the patient first in the intensive care unit before decompressing the urological system. On admission to the intensive care unit, we inserted an arterial line and central venous catheter, and did a bedside ultrasound, which showed a slit-like IVC, and continued fluid resuscitation was done. Initially, the patient responded well to fluid resuscitation, and noradrenaline was weaned to 0.06mcg/kg/min. Lactate also downtrended to 4.2. However, he became more hypotensive whenever fluid resuscitation was stopped. Eventually, he required noradrenaline and vasopressin to maintain his blood pressure. At this point, the urological team finally decided to proceed for the Priority 1 ureteroscopy and stenting. Intraoperatively, his blood pressure transiently worsened due to septic shower, requiring additional adrenaline infusion. This was weaned off after transfer back to the intensive care unit.", "points": [ { "heading": "Surviving sepsis guidelines and early source control", "text": "The latest surviving sepsis campaign guidelines from 2021 recommend an Hour-1 Bundle which should be initiated within an hour after sepsis recognition, and includes: lactate, blood culture, antibiotics, fluids and vasopressors if required. This aims to rapidly initiate antimicrobials while maintaining perfusion. However, early source control is still ideal, ideally within 6-12hours. Initial responsiveness to fluid and improving lactate may provide a false sense of security, and multidisciplinary discussions with the surgeon and operating theatre team should still be done to optimise the timing of source control and expedite where possible." }, { "heading": "Fluid responsiveness and POCUS-guided fluid administration", "text": "While the surviving sepsis guidelines recommend at least 30ml/kg of crystalloids in the first 3 hours, this is derived from “low quality” observational evidence. Conversely, it may lead to fluid overload and worsening oedema, worsening organ dysfunction, and requiring greater de-resuscitation subsequently. Instead, early vasopressor or inotropic support may be more suitable to maintain organ perfusion. Previous recommendations for early goal directed therapy  (e.g. CVP > 8, ScvO2 >70%) have been removed, but it is still important to find the balance between under-resuscitation and overload. Even with increasing number of newer tools such as POCUS, Flotrac and PCO2 gap, each has their own limitations, and a more accurate method would be to use a combination of techniques as well as clinical assessment, to be able to individualise fluid resuscitation to patient’s fluid responsiveness and fluid tolerance. Weighing all these factors is still challenging to me, but I have found that repeated POCUS and trending values in response to treatment to be the most useful method." } ], "references": [] }, { "headingId": "icu", "subId": null, "initials": "KF", "diagnosis": "End-of-life care and palliation in intensive care unit", "title": "Palliative Care In The Intensive Care Unit", "summary": "Patient was a 79-year-old lady with right thalamic haemorrhage with heavy clot burden. Her background history was ischaemic heart disease on dual antiplatelet therapy, previous ischaemic stroke with residual right sided weakness, hypertension, hyperlipidaemia and diabetes. She was mainly homebound with furniture cruising. Her GCS was E1V1M1 on arrival, and CT brain showed large thalamic intracerebral haemorrhage with midline shift and tonsillar herniation. She had an emergency clot evacuation and bilateral external ventricular drains placed. Sedation had been weaned off, and her GCS was still poor (E1V1M2). Electroencephalogram (EEG) showed no seizures. Neurosurgery opinion was of poor neurological prognosis, and were not keen to offer a tracheostomy. A family conference was held with both the intensive care team and neurosurgical team. After recapping her admission and brain injury, her current state and poor prognosis was explained. The son also shared that the patient did not want to suffer, and he felt that the way the patient was currently seemed like suffering. Decision was made for compassionate removal of mechanical ventilation. The patient was extubated the following day with the family members present, and passed away a few hours later.", "points": [ { "heading": "Palliative care and withdrawal of life support in the intensive care", "text": "It is important to consider the goals of care in patients who have poor prognosis and where further medical interventions may be futile or nonbeneficial. In elderly patients with multiple or severe irreversible organ failures, aggressive interventions may only serve to prolong suffering without providing meaningful quality of life. However, it is important to emphasize to family members that a transition from active life support to comfort care does not mean that we are “giving up” on the patient, but rather we will do all that we can to maximize the comfort of the patient." }, { "heading": "Communication with family and shared decision making in critical care", "text": "Shared decision making is an increasingly important part of medical care including critical care, especially involving withdrawal of life support. While patient autonomy should always be maximised, decision making of critical care patients who are too sick to convey their wishes often falls on family members who are presumed to have their best interests. However, family members are often unable to accept futility, and may delay the transition to comfort care. It is the medical team’s role to help family members come to terms with the reality of the situation, and multidisciplinary input and combined family conferences help to provide a unified front to help families come to terms. Achieving a consensus with the family through multiple family conferences is often the ideal balance of non-maleficence and the family’s wishes, and helps the patient pass away peacefully." } ], "references": [] }
];

